You are architecting and implementing a closed-source Minecraft mod called Sidequest.

Sidequest is primarily for a small private friend group playing Hypixel SkyBlock. It is not meant to compete publicly with SkyHanni, NEU, Firmament, Skytils, or similar mods. The goal is to combine personal progression features, friend-group utilities, achievements, cinematics, client-sided cosmetics, shared memories, and a self-hosted backend.

The config framework, custom UI framework, HUD system, layout engine, visual design, and HUD editor are already specified elsewhere. Do not redesign or re-architect them in this task. Treat them as existing systems with stable extension APIs.

The mod will eventually connect to a self-hosted backend and web dashboard running on a private homelab.

Do not start by implementing random features. First build the reusable runtime, data, networking, synchronization, event, storage, cosmetic, and diagnostic foundations that every feature will depend on.

The most likely ways this project fails are:

1. Every feature implements its own storage, event handling, networking, and parsing.
2. Minecraft-version-specific code leaks throughout the project.
3. Social features each invent their own synchronization format.
4. Cinematics, achievements, sounds, evidence, and cosmetics become disconnected systems.
5. Remote assets and backend features are implemented without strict security boundaries.
6. Too many one-off features create an unmaintainable private SkyHanni fork under a new name.

The architecture must prevent those failures.

# Product identity

Name: Sidequest

The mod is a private SkyBlock companion for:

- personal progression
- friend-group utilities
- achievements and joke progression
- client-sided cosmetics
- shared screenshots and incidents
- soundboard integrations
- synced waypoints and pings
- personal and group history

No public-facing tagline or marketing system is needed.

# Technical principles

Use Kotlin where practical.

Use a modular architecture.

Feature code must depend on stable Sidequest abstractions rather than raw Minecraft internals.

Every feature should register through a central feature context and automatically clean up:

- event listeners
- scheduled jobs
- network subscriptions
- commands
- temporary markers
- cached state

Avoid global mutable state where possible.

Prefer typed models, repositories, events, and service interfaces.

Do not let feature code directly:

- parse raw scoreboard text
- parse raw tab-list text
- perform HTTP requests
- open arbitrary WebSockets
- read/write JSON files
- download remote assets
- access Minecraft render internals
- resolve player identities by username alone

# Phase 1: core runtime utilities

## 1. Feature/module registry

Create a central feature framework.

Each feature should declare:

- stable feature ID
- display name
- category
- description
- supported Minecraft versions
- dependencies
- config section reference
- commands
- event listeners
- backend permissions
- network subscriptions
- optional experimental flag

Example style:

feature("rare_drop_animation") {
    category = Visuals
    config = RareDropConfig

    listen<RareDropEvent>(::onRareDrop)
    command("testraredrop", ::testRareDrop)
}

Disabling or unloading a feature must automatically unregister everything it created.

## 2. Typed event bus

Build a typed event system used by all features.

Requirements:

- typed event classes
- listener priorities
- cancellation where appropriate
- synchronous and asynchronous listeners
- main-thread dispatch support
- event tracing for debugging
- safe exception isolation
- event metadata such as timestamp and source
- feature-scoped listener registration

Core events should include:

- MinecraftJoinEvent
- MinecraftDisconnectEvent
- SkyBlockJoinEvent
- SkyBlockLeaveEvent
- IslandChangedEvent
- ServerChangedEvent
- ProfileChangedEvent
- ActivityChangedEvent
- DungeonStartedEvent
- DungeonEndedEvent
- KuudraStartedEvent
- KuudraEndedEvent
- CombatStateChangedEvent
- PlayerDeathEvent
- RareDropEvent
- SkillProgressEvent
- SkillLevelUpEvent
- AchievementUnlockedEvent
- TitleUnlockedEvent
- CurrencyChangedEvent
- FriendStatusChangedEvent
- FriendActivityChangedEvent
- PingCreatedEvent
- ReadyCheckStartedEvent
- ReadyCheckUpdatedEvent
- DebtUpdatedEvent
- CosmeticEquippedEvent
- EvidenceCreatedEvent
- CinematicQueuedEvent
- CinematicPlayedEvent
- SoundboardRuleTriggeredEvent

## 3. Scheduler and coroutine utilities

Provide:

- main-thread execution
- asynchronous IO
- delayed tasks
- repeating tasks
- debounce
- throttle
- timeout
- retry
- exponential backoff
- cancellation
- feature-scoped job ownership
- automatic cancellation when a feature unloads
- safe dispatch back to the Minecraft thread

## 4. Minecraft version abstraction

Hide version-specific Minecraft code behind stable interfaces.

Wrap:

- player access
- world access
- current screen
- item components/NBT
- entity data
- chat components
- sounds
- commands
- keybinds
- raycasting
- world interaction
- screenshots
- skins and capes
- client networking hooks

Keep version-specific implementations isolated.

## 5. Game context service

Create one authoritative service for current gameplay context.

It should expose:

- whether the user is in SkyBlock
- current Hypixel server
- current island
- current sub-location
- current profile
- current activity
- current dungeon state
- current Kuudra state
- current boss phase
- combat state
- inventory/screen state
- whether a cinematic is safe to display
- whether notifications should be reduced
- party state
- nearby custom friends
- current session state

Features must consume typed context instead of independently guessing state.

## 6. Chat parser registry

Build centralized chat classification.

Support:

- formatted and unformatted text
- component-aware matching
- regex registry
- pattern versioning
- duplicate suppression
- sender extraction
- clickable component inspection
- localization-ready matching
- debug logging
- parser fixtures and tests

Expose typed events instead of raw strings.

Examples:

- rare drop detected
- party invite
- ready-check response
- skill level-up
- debt confirmation command
- dungeon completion
- Kuudra completion
- friend join/leave
- auction event
- achievement trigger

## 7. Scoreboard parser

Provide:

- raw lines
- cleaned lines
- key/value extraction
- change detection
- island detection
- activity detection
- phase detection
- profile detection
- dungeon and Kuudra detection
- parser debugging

## 8. Tab-list parser

Provide:

- raw entries
- cleaned entries
- player extraction
- party extraction
- activity clues
- server clues
- change events
- parser debug output

## 9. Item abstraction

Create a stable persistent item model.

Do not persist raw ItemStack instances.

Store where available:

- SkyBlock ID
- item UUID
- display name
- rarity
- quantity
- lore snapshot
- texture or icon reference
- relevant upgrades
- enchantments
- estimated value
- acquisition context
- serialized safe snapshot

Use this model for:

- rare drops
- evidence
- debts
- item lending
- upgrade history
- cosmetics
- screenshots
- notifications

## 10. Player identity service

Use UUID as the canonical identity.

Support:

- UUID
- current username
- username history
- nickname
- skin reference
- custom friend membership
- online state
- activity
- location-sharing permissions
- profile cache
- cosmetics
- titles
- achievements
- debt summary

Never use username as the permanent database key.

## 11. Player targeting utilities

Support:

- raycast player under crosshair
- line-of-sight checks
- distance
- UUID resolution
- last-targeted player
- command-based fallback
- chat-name click target
- friend-list target
- opening player action GUI

## 12. Location and island abstraction

Provide typed models for:

- island
- sub-location
- world position
- server identifier
- dungeon room
- Kuudra area
- NPC position
- shared waypoint destination

Account for island-specific coordinate handling.

## 13. Party service

Expose:

- party members
- leader
- party state
- joins/leaves
- party activity
- ready-check state
- role assignments
- whether a user has Sidequest
- group-session state

Do not let features parse party chat independently.

## 14. Activity detection

Detect high-level activities such as:

- Dungeons
- Kuudra
- Mining
- Garden
- Farming
- Fishing
- Slayer
- Rift
- Crimson Isle questing
- Forge management
- General exploration
- Idle/unknown

Allow confidence levels and fall back to unknown instead of inventing state.

# Phase 2: persistence and backend

## 15. Persistence layer

Provide typed repositories for:

- global settings references
- account settings references
- SkyBlock profile data
- local cache
- friend-group data
- offline event queue
- achievement progress
- cosmetics
- evidence metadata
- debts
- waypoints
- notifications
- session history

Requirements:

- schema versions
- migrations
- atomic writes
- corruption detection
- fallback recovery
- backups
- validation
- migration tests

Features must not directly read or write JSON.

## 16. Backend API client

Centralize all HTTP access.

Support:

- authentication
- typed requests/responses
- retries
- timeouts
- exponential backoff
- error mapping
- caching
- rate limiting
- request IDs
- server-time synchronization
- offline mode
- queued writes
- API version negotiation

## 17. Authentication and device pairing

Use a secure pairing flow.

Example:

1. Mod requests a short-lived pairing code.
2. User logs into the Sidequest dashboard.
3. User enters the code.
4. Backend associates the device with the Minecraft UUID.
5. Mod receives a scoped device token.

Requirements:

- short-lived access tokens
- refresh tokens
- device revocation
- multiple devices
- session listing
- scoped permissions
- no use of Minecraft session tokens
- secure token storage

## 18. Realtime synchronization

Use one versioned realtime protocol, preferably over WebSocket.

It should carry:

- presence
- activity status
- friend updates
- pings
- waypoints
- ready checks
- debts
- payments
- achievements
- titles
- currency
- cosmetics
- evidence events
- comments
- reactions
- soundboard events
- dashboard updates

Every message should include:

- protocol version
- event type
- message ID
- sender UUID
- timestamp
- payload
- permission scope
- optional acknowledgement requirement

Support:

- reconnect
- replay/resume
- duplicate suppression
- acknowledgements
- offline queue
- stale-event expiry
- ordering where required

## 19. Permissions and privacy

Roles:

- owner
- admin
- member
- guest

Per-feature permissions should include:

- view online status
- view activity
- view island
- view exact position
- send pings
- send waypoints
- create ready checks
- create debts
- confirm payments
- upload evidence
- edit evidence
- assign titles
- assign joke cosmetics
- trigger synchronized sounds
- moderate group content
- create achievements
- manage shop rewards

Support per-user overrides.

# Phase 3: event presentation and automation

## 20. Notification integration

The notification UI already exists elsewhere.

Build the runtime manager that supplies it with:

- category
- priority
- icon reference
- title
- subtitle
- progress
- duration
- actions
- sound
- grouping key
- deduplication key
- history entry
- activity context
- display policy

Delivery modes:

- toast only
- inbox only
- both
- disabled
- compact while busy
- queue until safe

## 21. Cinematic event engine

Build one reusable cinematic runtime.

The visual implementation may consume the existing UI/render framework.

Possible components:

- item model
- player head or model
- title
- subtitle
- animated number
- progress bar
- rarity colour
- particles
- shader
- background
- letterbox bars
- sound
- voice clip
- screenshot
- reward reveal
- friend reactions

Event policies:

- show immediately
- show compactly
- queue
- merge
- log silently
- expire
- discard if stale

Unsafe contexts include:

- active Dungeon run
- active Kuudra run
- dangerous boss phase
- combat
- low health
- open inventory
- another cinematic
- loading screen
- disconnected state

Example:

Rare drop during Dungeons:
- log immediately
- optionally show compact notification
- queue full cinematic
- play after leaving Dungeons
- merge with other queued run events
- expire after configurable time

Queued events should support:

- priority
- maximum queue size
- expiry
- grouping
- deduplication
- recap mode
- manual replay
- skip
- queue indicator

## 22. Rule engine

Create a generic rule engine for:

- achievements
- soundboard triggers
