# Implementation order

Implement in this order:

1. Version abstraction
2. Feature/module registry
3. Event bus
4. Scheduler
5. Game context
6. Chat/scoreboard/tab parsers
7. Item/player/location abstractions
8. Persistence and migrations
9. Notification manager integration
10. Cinematic queue
11. Backend authentication
12. API client
13. WebSocket sync
14. Identity/friends/presence
15. Permissions
16. Asset manager
17. Waypoint/ping engine
18. Rule engine
19. Achievement system
20. Currency system
21. Cosmetic engine
22. Evidence system
23. Debt system
24. Friend/player GUIs using the existing UI framework
25. Dashboard
26. Move existing SkyHanni features
27. Add joke and secret content

Do not implement the entire project as a single pass.

For every phase:

- define interfaces first
- implement tests
- add debug inspection
- document extension points
- keep feature code independent
- avoid raw Minecraft dependencies outside adapters
- avoid backend calls outside backend services
- avoid persistence calls outside repositories
- avoid remote asset loading outside the asset manager

The end result should be a maintainable private mod platform where adding a new feature usually means:

- declaring config through the existing config framework
- listening to typed events
- storing typed state
- optionally registering an existing HUD/UI component
- optionally defining rules, rewards, cinematics, or sync messages

It should not require rebuilding networking, storage, rendering, or parsing for every new feature.